        "side": "new",
        "severity": "medium",
        "category": "bug",
        "title": "Any Value() in JSON key __in is now treated as JSON text, not just JSON Values",
        "explanation": "The new condition matches every `expressions.Value`, whatever its output_field. Take a plain `Value(\"bar\")`, which resolves to CharField and compiles to `%s` with params ['bar']. Before this change it was compared as-is to the extracted key, and that worked. Now on SQLite, 'bar' is not in jsonfield_datatype_values, so the code emits `JSON_EXTRACT(%s, '$')` on the raw string 'bar', which is not valid JSON, and SQLite raises a malformed-JSON error. MySQL does the same and raises 'Invalid JSON text'. On Oracle the code wraps it in `JSON_OBJECT(... VALUE %s FORMAT JSON)`, which is also invalid JSON. So a query like `value__foo__in=[Value(\"bar\")]` worked before and now fails. Only Values whose output_field is a JSONField hold JSON-encoded params, so only those should take this path.",
        "suggestion": "Limit the new branch to JSON-typed Values, e.g. `isinstance(param, expressions.Value) and isinstance(param.output_field, JSONField)`, and add a test with a non-JSON Value such as `Value(\"bar\")`.",
        "evidence": "not hasattr(param, \"as_sql\") or isinstance(param, expressions.Value)",
        "confidence": 0.65,
